<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Rohan Pandey
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Harry Richman
</span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper analyzes the Möbius (<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(i)</annotation></semantics></math>) function defined on the partially ordered set of triangular numbers (<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>) under the divisibility relation. We make conjectures on the asymptotic behavior of the classical Möbius and Mertens functions on the basis of experimental data and other proven conjectures. We first introduce the growth of partial sums of <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> and analyze how the growth is different from the classical Möbius function, and then analyze the relation between the partial sums of <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)|</annotation></semantics></math>, and how it is similar to the asymptotic classical Möbius function. Which also happens to involve the Riemann zeta function. Then we create Hasse diagrams of the poset, this helps introduce a method to visualize the divisibility relation of triangular numbers. This also serves as a basis for the zeta and Möbius matrices. Looking specifically into the poset defined by <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathbb{N},\leq_{\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{N},\leq_{\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math>, or triangular numbers under divisibility and applying the Möbius function to it, we are able create our desired matrices. And then using Python libraries we create visualizations for further analysis, and are able to project previously mentioned patterns. Through which we are able to introduce two more novel conjectures bounding <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and the sums of <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)}{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)}{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude the paper with divisibility patterns in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Triangular number divisibility patterns ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, with proofs of the helpful and necessary propositions.</p>
  
</div>
