</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Background</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Further information is included in this section to clarify terms and diagrams and other information.
For further background on number theory, see Burton <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.
For further background on the combinatorics of posets, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>The classical Möbius and Mertens functions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The classical Möbius function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>is defined, for a positive integer <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, by the following rules:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(n)=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }n=1,\\
(-1)^{r}&amp;\text{if $n=p_{1}p_{2}\cdots p_{r}$, where $p_{i}$ are distinct primes},\\
0&amp;\text{if }p^{2}\,|\,n~\text{ for some prime }p.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mtext>, where </mtext><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> are distinct primes</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for some prime </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(n)=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }n=1,\\
(-1)^{r}&amp;\text{if $n=p_{1}p_{2}\cdots p_{r}$, where $p_{i}$ are distinct primes},\\
0&amp;\text{if }p^{2}\,|\,n~\text{ for some prime }p.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The behavior of the Möbius function is fundamentally linked to the structure of prime numbers and prime factorization.
In broad terms, the Möbius function captures the multiplicative structure of the integer <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Studying the Möbius function under an additive perspective, i.e. what happens when <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, reveals the interplay between multiplication and addition on the positive integers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Mertens function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by taking partial sums of the Möbius function,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(n)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\mu(k)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(n)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\mu(k).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Many properties of this function were studied by Mertens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Significantly, the Riemann hypothesis is equivalent to the asymptotic bound</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(n)=O(n^{1/2+\epsilon})\qquad\text{for any }\epsilon&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for any </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(n)=O(n^{1/2+\epsilon})\qquad\text{for any }\epsilon&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Whether this bounds holds is currently open, and is a subject of active research.
Kotnik and van de Lune
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>
investigate the asymptotics of the Mertens function by numerical experiment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Posets and Hasse diagrams</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The classical definition of the Möbius function depends on the prime factorization of a positive integer, which in turn depends on the relation of integer divisibility.
Integer divisibility defines a <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">partial order</span> relation on the positive integers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The classical Möbius function is uniquely characterized by the following equations, coming from the integer divisibility relation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(M.1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(M.2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{d\,|\,n}\mu(d)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.448em">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{d\,|\,n}\mu(d)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These relations can be adapted to form the definition of the “Möbius function” for an arbitrary partially ordered set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Before discussing this generalized Möbius function, let us first recall some definitions.
A partially ordered set, also known as a <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">poset</span>, is a tool for ordering combinatorial objects.
For more background, see Bona <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Chapter 16]</cite>.
A poset <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\preccurlyeq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≼</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\preccurlyeq)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of an underlying set <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and a binary relation “<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\preccurlyeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\preccurlyeq</annotation></semantics></math>” on <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following axioms:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Reflexivity)
<math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\preccurlyeq x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\preccurlyeq x</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in S</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Antisymmetry)
if <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\preccurlyeq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\preccurlyeq y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\preccurlyeq x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\preccurlyeq x</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=y</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(Transitivity)
if <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\preccurlyeq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\preccurlyeq y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\preccurlyeq z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\preccurlyeq z</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\preccurlyeq z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\preccurlyeq z</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">If these hold, then we call
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\preccurlyeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\preccurlyeq</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S2.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">partial order relation</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hasse diagram</span> of a poset is a nice way to represent a poset graphically.
It is a directed graph whose vertices are the elements of the poset, and whose edges correspond to covering relations in the poset.
A <span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">covering relation</span> is a pair <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> of distinct elements such that <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\preccurlyeq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\preccurlyeq y</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is no element <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\not\in\{x,y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\not\in\{x,y\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\preccurlyeq z\preccurlyeq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\preccurlyeq z\preccurlyeq y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Rather than indicating the directions of edges with arrows, it is typical to display Hasse diagrams so that all edges implicitly point “upwards,” i.e. if <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\preccurlyeq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\preccurlyeq y</annotation></semantics></math> is a covering relation, then we draw the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex lower and the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>-vertex higher in the diagram.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2. Posets and Hasse diagrams ‣ 2. Background ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is an example of a Hasse diagram of the integers <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,20\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,20\}</annotation></semantics></math> under the divisibility relation.
With the divisibility relation, the covering relations are those of the form <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pn" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pn</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a prime number.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.07934v1/Hasse_Diagram_for_Integer_Divisibility.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/224;" width="357" height="224" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Hasse Diagram for Integer Divisibility</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Generalized Möbius functions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now return to the generalized Möbius functions on a poset.
For more details on the Möbius function, see Bona <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Chapter 16.2]</cite>.
In order to define the Möbius function for <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\preccurlyeq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≼</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\preccurlyeq)</annotation></semantics></math> we need one additional technical definition:
we say a poset <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\preccurlyeq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≼</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\preccurlyeq)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">locally finite</span> if for any <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, the set <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z:x\preccurlyeq z\preccurlyeq y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z:x\preccurlyeq z\preccurlyeq y\}</annotation></semantics></math> of elements between <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is finite.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Möbius function</span> of a locally finite poset <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\preccurlyeq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≼</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\preccurlyeq)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows:
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{S}:S\times S\to\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{S}:S\times S\to\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(GM.0)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{S}(x,y)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{S}(x,y)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\not\preccurlyeq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⋠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\not\preccurlyeq y</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(GM.1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{S}(x,x)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{S}(x,x)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in S</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(GM.2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if <math id="S2.I3.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\preccurlyeq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\preccurlyeq y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I3.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\neq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\neq y</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.I3.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{z:\,x\preccurlyeq z\preccurlyeq y}\mu_{s}(x,z)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{z:\,x\preccurlyeq z\preccurlyeq y}\mu_{s}(x,z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now suppose there is an element <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\preccurlyeq x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\preccurlyeq x</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Möbius function of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\preccurlyeq)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≼</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\preccurlyeq)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{S}(1,1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{S}(1,1)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{y\preccurlyeq x}\mu_{P}(1,y)=0\qquad\text{if }x\neq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{y\preccurlyeq x}\mu_{P}(1,y)=0\qquad\text{if }x\neq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Matrix computation: Zeta matrix and Möbius matrix</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now describe how the generalized Möbius function, defined in the previous section, has an equivalent description in terms of matrices.
This matrix version has the advantage of being straightforward to implement in a programming language, using a well-supported linear algebra library.
For the rest of the paper, we assume for convenience that our poset is defined on the underlying set <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">zeta matrix</span> of a poset <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P=(\mathbb{N},\preccurlyeq_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≼</mo><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(\mathbb{N},\preccurlyeq_{P})</annotation></semantics></math> on the natural numbers is the <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued matrix whose entries are</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i,j}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }j\preccurlyeq_{P}i,\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>≼</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i,j}=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }j\preccurlyeq_{P}i,\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Examples of these matrices using the triangular numbers and integers (for comparison) are shown in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Triangular numbers: zeta matrix and Möbius matrix ‣ 3. Poset of triangular numbers ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
Creating the zeta matrix for a poset
is equivalent to figuring out the poset relation between all pairs of elements in the poset;
to find a submatrix of the zeta matrix, we just need to figure out the poset relation between pairs of corresponding elements.
The <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row of the zeta matrix records which elements are larger than <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in the poset;
the <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of the zeta matrix records which elements are smaller than <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> in the poset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We may compute the Möbius function of a poset by finding the matrix inverse of the zeta matrix.
Namely, if <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a locally finite poset, let</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=Z^{-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=Z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the zeta matrix of <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> from above.
Then the entries of the matrix <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly the Möbius function values:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i,j}=\mu_{P}(j,i)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i,j}=\mu_{P}(j,i).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">This is because the properties defining the Möbius function, (GM.0 - GM.2) above, are equivalent to the matrix relation</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mu_{P}(i,j)]_{i,j\in\mathbb{N}}\,Z=I," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mu_{P}(i,j)]_{i,j\in\mathbb{N}}\,Z=I,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the infinite <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note: In this paper we focus on studying the first row of the Möbius matrix.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Poset of triangular numbers</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we define the poset which is the main focus of this paper.
As before, let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(n)=\frac{1}{2}n(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(n)=\frac{1}{2}n(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th triangular number.
By abuse of notation, let <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> also denote the set of <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">triangular numbers</span>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\left\{\textstyle\frac{1}{2}n(n+1):n=1,2,\ldots\right\}=\{1,3,6,10,\ldots\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\left\{\textstyle\frac{1}{2}n(n+1):n=1,2,\ldots\right\}=\{1,3,6,10,\ldots\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Consider the poset <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathbb{N},\leq_{\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{N},\leq_{\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> define by <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq j</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(j)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Hasse diagram</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we show the Hasse diagram of the first <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> elements of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathbb{N},\leq_{\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{N},\leq_{\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
which shows the divisibility relations among the first <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> triangular numbers,
in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1. Hasse diagram ‣ 3. Poset of triangular numbers ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
Recall that a line is drawn between two numbers if they are “minimally related” to one other.
For example, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(19)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>19</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(19)</annotation></semantics></math>, so there is a line from <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.07934v1/Hasse_Diagram_for_Triangular_Numbers.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/248;" width="357" height="248" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Hasse diagram for <math id="S3.F4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\leq_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, encoding triangular number divisibility.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It’s important to note that in the Hasse diagram, relations implied by transitivity are not shown.
For example, the relations <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5\leq_{\mathcal{T}}14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq_{\mathcal{T}}14</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="14\leq_{\mathcal{T}}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14\leq_{\mathcal{T}}20</annotation></semantics></math> are shown by edges in the Hasse diagram, since <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(5)</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(14)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(14)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(14)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(14)</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(20)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(20)</annotation></semantics></math>.
But even though <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="5\leq_{\mathcal{T}}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq_{\mathcal{T}}20</annotation></semantics></math>, we don’t have this edge in the Hasse diagram because this relation is already implied by
the upwards-path of edges from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From the Hasse diagram, we can visibly see that the poset <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\mathbb{N},\leq_{\mathcal{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{N},\leq_{\mathcal{T}})</annotation></semantics></math> is more disordered than the usual divisibility poset, shown earlier in Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2. Posets and Hasse diagrams ‣ 2. Background ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
We note some patterns in Section <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Triangular number divisibility patterns ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Triangular numbers: zeta matrix and Möbius matrix</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote Möbius function for the triangular numbers under the divisibility relation, in the sense defined in Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Generalized Möbius functions ‣ 2. Background ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>.
Namely, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique function that satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(1,1)=1,\qquad\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)=-\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}d\leq_{\mathcal{T}}n\\[1.63885pt]
d\neq n\end{subarray}}\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(1,d)\quad\text{for all }n\geq 2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(1,1)=1,\qquad\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)=-\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}d\leq_{\mathcal{T}}n\\[1.63885pt]
d\neq n\end{subarray}}\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(1,d)\quad\text{for all }n\geq 2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned earlier in Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Matrix computation: Zeta matrix and Möbius matrix ‣ 2. Background ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, the values of the Möbius function <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed by inverting the zeta matrix.
Each row of the zeta matrix records which elements are greater than a given element, in the poset relation, and each column records which elements are smaller.
For every relation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i\leq_{\mathcal{T}}j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq_{\mathcal{T}}j</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is inserted in the corresponding <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th row and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of the zeta matrix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following shows the initial part of the zeta matrix:</p>
