<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Data: Partial sums of Möbius values</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we show data on the partial sums of the Möbius values <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This sequence of partial sums is available at the OEIS entry A351167 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2. Data: Partial sums of Möbius values ‣ 4. Results: data on Möbius values ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a graph of these partial sums, for up to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10,000</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.07934v1/10000_Sum_Function.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:448/336;" width="448" height="336" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6. </span>Partial sums of Möbius function values.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1. Möbius values with 𝑚=1 ‣ 4. Results: data on Möbius values ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2. Data: Partial sums of Möbius values ‣ 4. Results: data on Möbius values ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows a clear downward trend.
This leads us to make the following conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture</span></span><span id="Thmconjx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Growth of partial sums of <math id="Thmconjx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmconjx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a positive constant <math id="Thmconjx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)\leq-Cn\quad\text{for all sufficiently large }n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for all sufficiently large </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)\leq-Cn\quad\text{for all sufficiently large }n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, this conjecture states that the average value of the Möbius function <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is eventually bounded above by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-C</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.</p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)\leq-C." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)\leq-C.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Data: Partial sums of Möbius value absolute values</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3. Data: Partial sums of Möbius value absolute values ‣ 4. Results: data on Möbius values ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> we show the partial sums of the absolute values <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)|</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this figure, the trend is even smoother than in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2. Data: Partial sums of Möbius values ‣ 4. Results: data on Möbius values ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2402.07934v1/1000_Sum_Abs_Value.PNG" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:576/373;" width="576" height="373" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7. </span>Partial sums of Möbius function absolute values <math id="S4.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(n)|</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This data in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3. Data: Partial sums of Möbius value absolute values ‣ 4. Results: data on Möbius values ‣ The Möbius function of the poset of triangular numbers under divisibility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> leads us to make the following conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture</span></span><span id="Thmconjx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Partial sums of <math id="Thmconjx2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}|</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmconjx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As <math id="Thmconjx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)|=\frac{1}{2}n+o(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)|=\frac{1}{2}n+o(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Average magnitude of Möbius values</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The conjecture can be rephrased in terms of the average magnitute of the the Möbius function values.
Namely, that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)|=\frac{1}{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}|\mu_{\mathcal{T}}(i)|=\frac{1}{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Möbius values with large magnitude</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe empirically that the values of the Möbius function <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> seem to achieve arbitrarily large magnitude.
This is in contrast with the classical Möbius function <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, which only has values in <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
